## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Metal organic frameworks are a kind of crystalline porous materials with periodic network structures formed by selfassembly of inorganic metal centers (metal ions or metal clusters) and bridged organic ligands. As a porous solid material, MOFs have become a shining star in many fields such as catalysis, adsorption, drug transportation and photoelectrics. Due to the tunable structure, more than 7000 kinds of topological structures have been reported. In the past ten years, the research on the self-assembly and related properties of MOFs has increased exponentially [1,2]. The self-assembly of MOFs is a comprehensive application of crystal morphology, coordination chemistry and chemical engineering. Also it is an important progress in the development of coordination polymers [3,4]. The MOFs are not limited to microporous structure and macroporous structure, which make the development of multiscale MOFs possible. In this manner, the crystal morphology and size can be adjusted to suit specific applications. In 2007, Fischer et al. [5] applied time-resolved static light scattering (SLS) to synchronously monitor the hydrothermal self-assembly process of MOF-5 by p-perfluoromethylbenzoic acid. In 2008, a breakthrough has been made in the application of coordination modulation in the self-assembly of Zr-base MOFs. The coordination modulator can effectively increase the crystallinity and size of crystals [6]. With structures solved from powder X-ray diffraction data, it was shown that modulation could dramatically enhance crystallinity and particle size of Zr MOFs of the UiO-66 isoreticular series. In 2009, Kitagawa et al. [7] first introduced the concept of coordination modulation into the self-assembly of MOFs. In 2010, Guo et al. [8] concerned the self-assembly and simple synthesis of Dy(BTC) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocrystals using sodium acetate as a capping agent. In 2012, they then investigated the effects of different amounts and types of capping agents and the solution pH on the nanocrystalline synthesis [9]. The coordination modulation strategy can effectively act on nanometer sized crystals. Umemura et al. [10] have recently reported the crystal size and morphology are controlled by ndodecanoic acid and lauric acid, and the transformation of octahedron, trishedron, and cubic crystal is controlled by modulators. It was discussed that different modulators controlled crystal growth in the [100] and [00 1] direction, acid modulators affected the coordination between copper and ndc, and amine played an important role in the coordination between copper and dabco [11]. Therefore, the control of crystal size and morphology should take the comprehensive effects of modulators with different properties into account.
In recent years, it has become a research hotspot to introduce defects into MOFs by means of coordination adjustment [12,13]. In 2013, the high resolution neutron power diffraction technique provides strong evidence for the missing linker defect, which was reported by Wu et al. [14]. Missing linker is considered as an effective method for increasing porosity and defects. They found that the porosity of UiO-66 can be further enhanced by tuning the missing-linker defects. In 2014, reliable evidence was provided by Goodwin and co-workers for the existence of missing cluster defects in UiO-66, formic acid reduced the number of connections of the ideal UiO-66 from 12 to 8 and converted it into reo crystalline phase [15]. In 2016, the study of G.C. Shearer et al. [16] provided evidence for the missing clusters defect modulated by monocarboxylic acid. Some useful conclusions can be drawn: (1) The missing cluster defects account for a larger proportion than the missing ligand defects modulated by monocarboxylic acid; (2) The concentration of missing cluster defects is affected by the amount and acidity of the modulator; (3) The defects are compensated by a combination of DMF decomposition product formic acid and deprotonated modulator molecules. The defect modulation of MOFs is influenced by the synthesis conditions, synthesis temperature, the acidity and concentration of the modulator, and the ratio of modulator to ligand.
## 2. The classification of modulator
Generally, crystal nucleation is divided into three stages: (i) an induction period, where the particles with the critical size are formed; (ii) a nucleation period, where primary particles evolve to the crystal nuclei; (iii) a growth period, where molecules are arranged on the surface of existing nuclei followed by subsequent growth into larger crystal [20]. A schematic diagram representing evolution of each period, in terms of relative crystallinity versus time is represented in Fig. 3. According to the influence of preparation on nucleation rate and nucleation process, the modulator can be divided into coordination modulator and deprotonation modulator.
## 2.1. Coordination modulator
## 2.1.1. Monodentate carboxyl acid modulator
The term ’’coordination modulation ’’ was first introduced by the group of Kitagawa into the synthesis of $\mathrm{|\{Cu_{2}(N D C)_{2}(d a b c o)\}|}$ ${\mathfrak{n}}_{6}$ and HKUST-1 crystals. Coordination modulation means to the use of organic acids or bases that form a competitive relationship with ligands to play the role of coordination modulation, breaking the coordination balance in the process of crystal growth [21]. In 2011, Behrens and co-workers investigated the guiding effect of monocarboxylic acid on self-assembly of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based MOFs [22]. It was meaningful that the modulator was an important factor affecting the self-loading of UiO-66. During the self-assembly process, the coordination modulator competes with the ligands for the Zr coordination site, resulting in the termination of the coordination bonding of the ligand, or the coordination modulator may participate in the coordination bonding of metal clusters.
In 2012, Wißmann, G., et al. [23] reported that formic acid was indispensable in the synthesis of Zr-fumarate MOF. The high concentration of zirconium fumarate promoted the rapid emergence of white precipitate, $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ was sufficient for crystal synthesis. As the decomposition product of solvent DMF, formic acid played an important role in accelerating the crystal formation process (Fig. 4).
The presence of formic acid as a modulator could displace this equilibrium to the left side so that more water is available in the reaction system. Water is essential for the hydrolysis of the Zr precursor and for the supply of oxygen atoms for the formation of the $\mathrm{{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{{O}_{4}(0H)_{4}(0_{2}C)_{12}}}$ SBU. Thus, formic acid could indirectly enhance the formation of the SBU and thus acceleration of the formation of the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -fumarate MOF.
In 2014, the study on the formation of Zr-fumarate MOF by in situ synchrotron diffraction was reported by Zahn, G., et al. [24]. The energy-dispersive diffraction was applied to real-time monitoring of Zr-fumarate coordination regulation process. For water reaction, the nucleation of crystals is slowed down due to the interference of formic acid. For DMF solvent reaction, the modulator accelerated the nucleation of crystals, leading to the emergence of nano-sized crystals. Therefore, the effect of the modulation should be considered comprehensive with the solvent effect.
In 2015, the coordination modulation of amino acids has received much attention. Amino acid modulates the Zr-MOFs in the same way as benzoic and formic acid do [25]. When 5 equivalents of the l-proline modulator was applied, high yields in ZrMOFs was achieved. The spatial effect of l-proline provides the conditions for obtaining high quality crystals. When the ratio of amino acid to fumaric acid was 1:1, amino acid was considered to introduce the defective sites of Zr-MOFs. A wide range of amino acids was involved, and N-formylproline acid and pyroglutamic acid favored the appearance of small crystals due to the involvement of the $\mathsf{N H}^{2+}$ and $C O_{2}\mathrm{H}$ moieties [26].
In 2020, the acid modulators were considered to have significant effects on crystal nucleation and growth due to the increased solubility of 2-aminoterephthalic acid ligand by virtue of protonation of the amine groups. The acid modulator suppressed the deprotonation of $H_{2}N-H_{2}B D C$ ligand, which is also found in the synthesis of MIL-101(Cr)– $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanoparticles [27].
## 2.1.2. Hydrochloric acid modulator
Hydrochloric acid was thought to accelerate MOF synthesis kinetics and precipitation favors the formation of defects through misconnections or linker dislocation. For hydrothermal reactions, the solubility of ligand was improved effectively and the particle aggregates was increased by the deprotonation of the $H_{2}N-H_{2}B D C$ ligand. Deprotonation modulation of hydrochloric acid is equally important. An interesting finding is that HCl can accelerate the synthesis of UiO-66 and its analogues [28]. Existing studies have shown that hydrochloric acid can not only accelerate the synthesis of the UiO-66, but also modulate the amino functionalization UiO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , UiO-66- $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})_{2}$ , hydroxyl functionalized UiO-66-OH, UiO-66- $(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and nitro functionalized UiO-66- ${\cdot\mathsf{N O}}_{2}$ . The functions of HCl can be summarized as follows: Firstly, HCl modulates the solubility of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ and accelerates the formation of crystals. Secondly, the neutralization effect occurred between hydrochloric acid and amine. The presence of water in the solvent has an influence on the crystal crystallization process. The modulation action should be considered comprehensive with the action of solvent.
The introduction of defects into the UiO-66 modulated by HCl is concomitant with the chloride ions introduced into the nodes as charge compensators, which has a vital effect on the crystal morphology and properties [29]. In the study of C.A. Clark et al., the UiO-66 modulated by HCl showed excellent adsorption removal of perfluorooctanesulfonate [30]. The decrease of hydrophobicity caused by coordination modulation may be one of the reasons that affect the adsorption capacity. Both missing linker defects and missing node defects generate linker deficiencies on the Zr nodes of UiO-66 materials, which have been directly correlated with the decreased hydrophobicity of the MOF.
## 2.2. Deprotonation modulator
## 2.2.1. Carboxylate salt modulator
Different from the competitive action of carboxyl acid ligands, there are also a class of modulator played the role of deprotonation. The classic case of the ZIF-8 nanocrystals was confined in nanosize by the carboxylate, N-heterocycle, alkylamine modulator [31]. The loss of modulators leads to synthesis of amorphous ZIF-8, while the supplement of sodium formate, 1-Methylimidazole, nbutylamine is the key to obtain pure ZIF-8. The modulator restricted the ZIF-8 rhombododecahedron to a narrow size range. The modulator not only competed for the ligand in the metal center, but also facilitated the deprotonation of the bridge ligand. By means of coordination equilibrium and deprotonation equilibrium, the favorable conditions for crystal synthesis were found. The time-resolved in situ X-ray diffraction was introduced to study the deprotonation function of formate [32], as shown in Fig. 5.
Sodium acetate which was interfered with the initial nucleation of the crystal leads to the synthesis of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) nanoparticles through deprotonating 2-aminoterephthalic acid, and the combination of sodium acetate and sodium hydroxide enhances the modulation effect [33]. Wang, F., et al. reported he controlled regulation of HKUST-1 with binuclear copper structure crystal by sodium formate, triethylam (TEA) as well as sodium acetate [34]. Carboxylate salts such as sodium formate and sodium acetate played the role of capping agent in the synthesis of NMOFs. The bi-functionality of sodium formate in reducing the particle size has been revealed. On the one hand, sodium formate increases the crystallization rate due to the weak alkalinity, on the other hand, sodium formate acts as a capping agent to reduce the number of nucleus. Xin et al also found that coordination modulation of nanoscale HKUST-1 by sodium acetate and triethylamine [35]. Sodium acetate, playing the role of capping agent, is also effective for the synthesis of Dy (BTC)– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanocrystalline [9]. On the one hand, sodium acetate effectively modulates the pH of the solution, making the solution environment conductive to the self-assembly of crystals [36], on the other hand, the growth of crystal face in the direction confirms the role of sodium acetate as a capping agent [111].
## 2.2.2. Basic modulator
In the study of Yang et al. [37], aqueous ammonia ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\boldsymbol{0}}$ and triethylamine (TEA) were selected as an alkaline modulator to reduce SUMOF-3 size, which was beneficial to the protonation of the organic ligand $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{N D C}$ . When $0.1\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of ${\sf N H}_{3}{\cdot}{\sf H}_{2}0$ was added, small SUMOF-3 crystals appear. When $0.3\mathrm{\mmol}$ ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{0}$ was added, this increased solution alkalinity leads to the emergence of nanocrystals. Like acid modulators, basic modulators play an important role in modulating the topological structure and specific morphology of crystals.
In 2018, the influence of TEA deprotonation and formic acid modulation on nucleation and growth of UiO-66 was reported by Shan, B., et al [38]. In their work, TEA was thought to influence the deprotonation process of ligands [39]. The composition of the solution is very complex, including Zr, ligand, modulator, deprotonated ligands, deprotonated modulator, as shown in Fig. 6. The deprotonated ligands are derived from the accumulation of protons in solution. Deprotonation modulators and coordination modulators play different roles in crystal growth, as shown in Fig. 7a. A DMF-hexane biphase system is designed to describe the directed movement and deprotonated of TEA as shown in Fig. 7b. During the synthesis, TEA flows from the upper phase (hexane) into the lower phase (DMF) and suppressed the number of partially deprotonated ligands. Interestingly, the two sides of the membrane shows different morphologies. The upper side of the membrane is coarsely covered by loose nanoparticles, while the lower side is tightly connected by symbiotic crystal layers.
Ammonia played a non-negligible modulated role in the bottom-up synthesis of 2D Co-based MOF nanosheets [40]. When the $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}/\mathsf{b i m}$ $\mathrm{{.bim}}=$ benzimidazole) molar ratio increased to 2, the bulk crystals are stacked in order to form a cubic morphology with a particle size of $5{-}8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . With an increase in the amount of $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathsf{O H}$ in the precursor solution, $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{b i m})_{4}$ crystals are separated into two-dimensional thin nanosheets. The colloidal properties were confirmed by the Tynadall effect. Urea is needed in the microwave solvent thermal synthesis of MIL-53(Al). The ammonium cations derived from the urea hydrolysis trigger the deprotonation modulation of fumarate ligands, leading to the MIL-53(Al) crystal formation [41].
Basic modulators including hexadecyl trimethyl ammonium bromide (CTAB), triethylamine (TEA) and tetramethylammonium nitrate (TMAN) played an important role in the directional growth of In-NDC MOF particles as reported by Bao et al.[42]. As shown in Scanning Electron Micrographic (SEM) images, highly uniform and monodispersed ellipsoid-like particles with size around $1.8\mathrm{-}2~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ in length and $0.6\substack{-1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in width were obtained by adding CTAB, TEA and TMAN, respectively. Another typical phenomenon is that the addition of basic modulator caused immediate white precipitates when the temperature reached $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Actually, acids and bases play different roles in coordination modulation, nucleation and crystallization of crystals. The basic modulator affects the deprotonation process of the ligand, and the accelerated crystallization process produces a large number of tiny crystal seeds. By contrast, the coordination modulation of acid is more obvious. Addition of basic n-butylamine: $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n-BuNH}_{2}}$ helps to improve the yield and nanocrystalline of ZIF-71. To obtain the desired morphology and size, Springer, S., et al.[43] considered addition of a comparatively strong base, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nBuNH}_{2}}$ , via the deprotonation of the 4,5-dichloroimidazole (Hdcim) linker molecules should create the deprotonation modulation balance is in favor of the product of ZIF-71 and improve the yield. And the appearance of the small crystals was due to the rapid crystallization process of crystals.
## 3. The effect of modulator on crystal structure
## 3.1. The effect of modulator on crystal morphology
The customized size and morphology of the MOFs is of great importance for the practical application. Thus, the researchers do their best to obtain the desired morphology and size to meet the practical application needs. It was found that the effect of modulators affects the crystallization and nucleation of crystals. With the modulator or additives, the products would be highly reproducible with good uniformity. In general, the modulator seeks balance between promoting crystal nucleation and inhibiting crystal growth [44].
The overall effect of the modulator on the crystal morphology was given from the point of view of the growth rate of the crystal surface [45]. Currently, the available research results indicate that the morphology of crystal depends on the relative growth rate of each crystal surface, namely, the differentiation degree of each crystal surface growth. The growth rates of orientations are proportional to the attachment energy of growth units. Metal nanocrystals can be approximately described as a spherical model, but, the MOFs are difficult to build a model because of complicated topologies. Umemura, A., et al. [46] recently induced rod-like crystal morphology in the tetragonal crystal system, $[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{n d c})_{2}$ $\left({\mathsf{d a b c o}}\right)\mathbf{\bar{l}_{n}},$ where two different coordination bonds participated in self-assembly of the MOFs (ndc $=1{,}4$ -naphthalenedicarboxylate; dabco $=1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo-[2.2.2]octane). The coordination between copper and $\mathtt{C u}$ -ndc was affected by the use of n-dodecanioc acid and lauric acid (LA), introducing the anisotropic crystal growth [47]. With an increase in the amount of lauric acid, the evolution of crystal morphology was obvious: octahedron (LA-50, $4.75\mathrm{\mmol}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ); cuboctahedron (LA-75, $7.13\mathrm{\mmol}$ ), truncated cube (LA-100, 9.5 mmol), and cube (LA-125, 11.88 mmol), as shown in Fig. 8a. The co-effect of slower and faster growth contributed to the morphology of MOFs. The dominant six square facets of {100} contributed to a cube, whereas eight triangle facets of {111} lead to an octahedron. A cuboctahedron was deemed as a transition from a cube and an octahedron. When the growth rate of {1 0 0} crystal face was greater than that of {1 1 1} crystal faces, the crystal is covered by {1 1 1} face with slower growth rate. Similarly, when $\mathsf{v}\{111\}>\mathsf{v}\{100\}$ , a cube will be produced. When the growth rates were similar, the transition cuboctahedron was appeared. The first introduction of the coarse-grained model reveals the role of the regulator. As shown in Fig. 8b, two site types, THREE and FOUR, became the dominant numbers of connectivity to produce 2D nucleation on the {111} and {100} faces, respectively [48]. Indeed, the evolution of crystal morphology was controlled by the energy difference between the THREE and FOUR sites. When $\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{THREE}}>\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{FOUR}},$ the simulated morphology became an octahedron, $\begin{array}{r c l}{\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{THREE}}}&{=}&{\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{FOUR}}}\end{array}$ a truncated octahedron, $\Delta E_{\mathrm{THREE}}<\Delta E_{\mathrm{FOUR}}$ a cuboctahedron, and $\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{THREE}}\ll\Delta\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{FOUR}}\ a$ cube. The effects of acetic acid and pyridine modulator on the Cu-ndc and Cu-dabco in $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{2}(n d c)_{2}(d a b c o)]_{n}}$ was studied. The formation of the nanocubes was attributed to the coordination modulation of pyridine [48]. The coordination bonding between copperdabco was affected, and the crystal growth in the [001] direction was suppressed, whereas the coordination modulation of acetic acid was found in modulating the copper-ndc mode on the four remaining $(h00)$ and $(0~\mathrm{k}0)$ facets and thus affected the growth of the crystal in the direction of [100]. As a result, the crystal growth in both the [001] and [100] directions and nanocube crystal morphology were controlled by the combined modulation of acetic acid and pyridine.
The appropriate modulator dosage seeks the right balance between size and morphology. Sodium acetate modulation enabled Fe-MIL-88 conversion from spindle, octahedron, bipyramidal hexagonal to octahedron. Without any capping agent, the Fe-MIL-88 presented the uniform octagonal morphology with the relatively low surface free energy [49]. When sodium acetate is supplied, the spindle-shaped Fe-MIL-88 appeared, further the increased concentration of the modulator was beneficial to the appearance of nanocrystals. Alternatively, glycerol instead of sodium acetate was supplied, the Fe-MIL-88 with bipyramidal hexagonal shape and good crystallinity was found. Meanwhile, the dispersibility and crystallinity of Fe-MIL-88 nanoparticles were improved because of the hydroxyl groups of the crystals in separate places [50].
The influence of modulators on crystal growth kinetics is also an important aspect for crystal morphology and size. The selfassembly and optimization of UiO-66(Zr) modulated by hydrochloric acid and water were firstly investigated by time-resolved in situ energy-dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction [51]. The most intense (1 1 1) Bragg peak was increased, suggesting that the crystal crystallization rate was increased and might be related to the easier formation of the ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ oxo/hydroxo clusters. The water as solvent has different effects by HCl modulator. If water favors the formation of the oxoclusters, the low concentration of the acid leads to a low reaction rate which may be due to the deprotonation of the ligand [52]. The choice of metal source is also important to improve the reusability of the method and the yield of the product.
## 3.2. The effect of modulator on crystal size
Crystal size is a very important factor for crystal applications. Especially when the particle size reaches the nanoscale, the nanometer size effect appears, such as the fluorescence properties of quantum dots and the fascinating catalytic properties of metal nanoparticles.
In 2010, Diring, S. p., et al. [53] developed a coordination modulation method to synthesize MOFs nanocrystals. The crystal size is controlled by the modulators competing with the framework linker [54]. Although the coordination modulation can effectively control the crystal size, it cannot effectively control the uniformity of nanoparticles. Although they succeeded in controlling the crystal morphology by the coordination modulation method, the fine-tuning of the crystal size still remains to be achieved. The coordination modulation method was used for the microwave-assisted synthesis of $\left[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}\right]$ modulated by ndodecanoic acid. The crystal size from $20\ \mathrm{nm}$ globular particles up to $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ cubic crystals is obvious, meanwhile, the high crystallinity and high adsorbability of nanocrystals were confirmed from the quantitative relationship between adsorption capacity and porosity. High concentration of the regulator slowed the nucleation of the crystal, whereas low concentration of the regulator promoted the synthesis of nanometer sized crystals (Fig. 9a). The coordination modulation is an effective strategy to obtain the nanocrystals by altering the coordination equilibrium at the crystal surface through competitive interactions [55,56]. On the one hand, the binding energy of the crystal surface is unneglectable when the crystal size reaches nanometers. On the other hand, the crystal size is reduced to nanometer, and the shortened diffusion distance is beneficial to the catalytic reaction and adsorption reaction.
In 2011, the coordination modulation effect of 1-methylimidazole (1-MeIm) on ZIF-8 was studied by Cravillion et al. [57] to control the particles size within a narrow nanometer size range. In 2018, the modulation effect of 1-MeIm on the crystal size and defect structure of Ce-MOF (BIT-58) [58] were reported. Based on the high crystallinity, the particle size was adjusted to about $30\mathrm{nm}$ . In particular, it is found that most nitrogenous heterocyclic ligands can have an obvious effect on crystal size, while the carboxyl ligands show little particle-size-decreasing effect. In 2020, Yu, Y. et al. [59] showed that the coordination modulation of 1-methylimidazole was also effective for ZIF-4. Thus the size and morphology, defect degree and crystallinity of the crystal are affected by 1-methylimidazole. In the synthetic solution, 1-methylimidazole and imidazole coexist, among which, the coordination modulation of 1-methylimidazole affects the nucleation and growth of crystals. Hence, due to lack of adequate ligand competitors 1-methylimidazole, excess linker imidazole leads to a rapid deprotonation reaction of $\scriptstyle[Z\ n({\mathrm{HIm}})]^{2+}$ and linker exchange between different $\left[Z\mathrm{n(HIm)}_{\mathrm{m}}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{n}}\right]$ complex, then generating fast nucleation and crystal growth of ZIF-4 nanocrystal, by contrast, the lack of the kinetics controller resulted in small irregular coarse crystals (Fig. 9b). As well as crystal growth, the crystal size was downsized to nanoscale, giving the material incredible properties and applications. However, it should be clearly realized that coordination adjustment is one of the effective ways to adjust the size of MOFs.
## 3.3. The effect of modulator on crystal defective structure
UiO-66 frameworks include two types of defects: ’’missing linker defect’’ and ’’missing cluster defect’’ [60,61]. The high resolution neutron power diffraction technique provides strong evidence for the missing linker defect, which was reported by Wu et al. [62]. In the common self-assembly process of MOFs, the number of inorganic nodes are 4,5,6, the missing linker would more likely to make framework or holes collapse, forming a grain boundary type defect instead of linker vacancy. Missing linker is considered as an effective method for increased porosity and defects. It was found that the missing ligand defect caused be coordination modulation is a method to improve the porosity of UiO-66. The 3 equivalent of acetic acid can increase the porosity and specific surface area of UiO-66 to $0.65~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and $1620~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ The neutron inelastic spectrum clearly showed the number of methyl groups that act as defect compensators.
Missing cluster defects were first reported by the Goodwin team [63]. They provided ample evidence that the use of formic acid modulators could reduce the number of UiO-66 connections from 12 to 8. Formate anions derived from FA modulator can be effectively used as a defect compensator for charge balance. On the other hand, the concept of missing linkers was proposed by Zhou et al [64]. They showed that acetic acid effectively modulates the porosity of UiO-66 through cluster defects. A similar phenomenon has been observed by Vermoortele et al. [65], who synthesized the defective UiO-66 modulated by trifluoroacetic acid. In 2016, the study of G.C. Shearer et al [66] provided evidence for the missing clusters defect modulated by monocarboxylic acid.
The quantitative characterization of defect structure has aroused wide interests among researchers. TGA is regarded as a method to quantitatively characterize defects. The theory behind this is as follows: if the theoretical weight loss plateau attributed to the linker based as on the molecular weight of the structure is higher than the experimental one calculated based on TGA, then this difference in weight loss is attributed to missing linkers in the framework that could quantitatively describe the number of defects in the structure [67]. Observing the TGA curves of formic acid and acetic acid modulated UiO-66, it is noticed that the experimental weigh loss attributed to the linker combustion is clearly lower than the theoretical one. This means that all of the acidmodulated samples are linker-deficient and have structural defects. Hu et al. [68] paid attention to the influence of modulator with different acidity on crystal defect structure. Defective structures are mainly due to the absence of ligands during the selfassembly of secondary building units. Interestingly, for the same preparation, HfFMA tends to have more defects, which can be attributed to the slow reaction kinetics of Hf salts leading to incomplete crystallization. Secondly, PXRD is an effective strategy to identify defective structures. In the study of G.C. Shearer [66], acid modulated UiO-66 contains a very broad peak spanning a 2h range of $2-7^{\circ}$ , where we confidently assign it to reo nanoregions. The reo phase can be thought as UiO-66 with one-quarter of its clusters missing. PXRD traces in L-lactic acid modulated samples, were highly crystalline and the increase in defect concentration did not have an effect in peak positions or related intensities. In order to confirm higher defect concentrations with increasing monocarboxylic acid concentration, we compared full width at half maxima (fwhm) values. It is found that there was an increase in fwhm with increasing molar ratio of L-lactic acid. The increase in fwhm contributes to the hypothesis that the high concentration of modulator corresponds to the high defect. Thirdly, NMR was also used to study defect structures. 1HNMR spectra were recorded and used to determine the relative concentration of L-lactic acid, terephthalic acid, and DMF in the frameworks by integration of selected peaks. The expected ratio of 0.85:0.5 of L-lactic acid/ terephthalic acid was not observed for 0.85Lac. Instead, there was a slight increase from 0.5Lac to 0.85Lac. Slater, B., et al.[68] attributed this to missing terephthalate linkers in the framework and calculated that there were 1 in 20 missing terephthalate linkers in 0.85Lac. Another classic example, the 1HNMR spectra recorded on the acetic acid modulated samples after digestion in
1 M NaOH (in ${\sf D}_{2}0$ ) is displayed in the study of G.C. Shearer. There are only three signals that have been confidently assigned to $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ , acetate, and formate. This is an early indication that monocarboxylates (both formate and acetate) indeed compensates for the defects in these samples. For example, the modulation of trifluoroacetic acid and hydrochloric acid was used in aqueous solvents, and the hydrolysis of trifluoroacetic acid needs to be taken into account. The second step was to activate the synthetic material to remove the unstable defect compensating molecules. For theoretical calculations, BDC ligands, hydrolochloric acid and trifluoroacetic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were considered. With respect to defect-free reference state A(X(BDC), the free energy of all the possible structures was analyzed (Fig. 10a). The decomposition of the free energy into enthalpic and entropic helps explain the probability of defect occurrence [69]. It is a thermodyanmic control process of the modulator cluster defects formation. The introduction of coordination modulator and the presence of electron-donor groups contributed to the reduced free energy barrier for formation of linker deficiencies (Fig. 10b).
## 3.4. The effect of modulators on crystal phase transition
The coexistence of many topological structures makes it very difficult to prepare pure phase MOFs. Coordination modulator can modulate the dynamic process of MOFs self-assembly by competing ions and protons, which is beneficial to improve the phase purity of crystal. In 2016, the modulator was successfully used in the synthesis of MOF-525, MOF-545 and PCN-223 nanocrystals [70]. The high acidity of acetic acid gives priority to the synthesis of MOF-525 $(25\%)$ and PCN-223 $(11\%)$ . The higher acidity of benzoic acid adjusted the distribution of MOF-525 $(16\%)$ and PCN223 $(16\%)$ . Furthermore, the modulation of formic acid $\mathrm{{'pK}_{a}}=3.77\mathrm{{'}}$ was more balanced in the distribution of MOF-525 $(5\%)$ , MOF-545 $(5\%)$ , and PCN-223 $(5\%)$ . It seems that too high acidity does not favor the formation of crystals. Under the investigation of Shaunak M. Shaikh et al [71], the influence of the modulators including aromatic modulators, small aliphatic modulators and long chain saturated carboxylic acids on self-loading of MOF-525 was investigated. Short-chain carboxylic acids are beneficial for the formation of PCN-222 and PCN-223. The crystallinity and phase purity of MOF-525 were improved by long chain carboxylic acid. When the myristic and strearic acid were chosen, high crystallinity and high purity of MOF-525 samples were obtained. The purity of MOF-525 can be improved by using only a small amount of aromatic modulator. On the other hand, the concentrations of deprotonated modulator and fully deprotonated TCPP $([\mathsf{M o d^{-}}]/[\mathsf{T C P P^{4-}}])$ are another important factors which affect the phase purity. The crystal growth rate and hydrolysis of metal source can be changed by modulator, which is an effective way to improve crystal purity [72]. It is found that PCN-221 and PCN-223 tend to grow in a mixed phase with irregular sizes and shapes when $Z\mathrm{r}0{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ as metal source in a conventional solvothemal reaction. When $0.3~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid was supplied, small globular rod was formed and known as PCN-221. When $0.7~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid was supplied, MOF-525 with a clear cubic morphology was identified. Besides, when $0.9~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid was supplied, PCN-221 in the polyhedron shape was identified.
The increased phase purity meant more active metal sites [73]. Benzoic acid and more rigid biphenyl-4-carboxylic acid played an important role in the polymorphisms transformation between NU-1000 and NU-901 [74]. More rigid biphenyl-4-carboxylic acid in replace of benzoic acid leads to a stronger steric interaction between the modulator coordinating on the $\scriptstyle0\times0-Z\Gamma_{6}$ node and misaligned nodes or linkers, which was helpful for the phase-pure NU1000. Slight distortion of the crystal structure channel is also reflected in the benzoic acid-regulated DUT-52, DUT-53 and DUT-84 [75]. Such a phenomenon can be explained by the repulsion effect of the bulky phenyl rings of BC and linker molecules that is reflected in the different O-Hf-O angles: 74.85(15)(tetragonal phase) and 74.44(18)(orthorhombic phase). This observation provides reliable evidence for the formation of modulators guided structure.
The emerging examples make it clear that coordination modulation is a powerful structure-oriented protocol that facilitates the selection of phase pure MOFs. The role of the modulator was also affected by the synthesis method and temperature, so it is necessary to consider the influence of multiple comprehensive factors on the crystal self-assembly.
## 3.5. The effect of modulators on crystal stability
The influence of coordination modulator on the stability of MOFs is worthy of attention. Stability determines practical application. In the study of N.Assad et al.[76], trifluoroacetic acid and TFAmodulated UiO-66 improved the adsorption capacity of arsenate. The acidity of the modulator also has an effect on the thermal stability of the MOFs, which was reflected in the TGA curve. In the TGA curve, the weight lessness of the first stage is mainly decreased by the evaporation of water. The second step is the dehydroxylation of the zirconium cluster and elimination of the monocarboxylate linkers. The third weight decrease step of UiO66–00 and UiO-66-36AA starts from $390^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , where as it was found to be $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for UiO-66-12AA and $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for the remaining three samples (UiO-66-100AA, UiO-66-12TFA, and UiO-66-36TFA). In the study of Vo, T. K., et al.[77], the UiO’66 weightless is also divided into three stages. It showed that HCl equivalents affected the weight loss of the UiO-66(Zr) samples. Defect-free structured UiO-66 is fully coordinated with 12 terephthalate linkers. As reported, the TGA analyses of UiO-66(Zr) can be used to estimate the average number of linkers in the secondary building units (SBUs) of UiO-66(Zr). As the amount of HCl equivalents was increased from 40 equiv to 80 equiv, 120 equiv, and 180 equiv, the mass loss becomes smaller due to the existence of defective structure. The framework decomposition weight loss step involved the complete combustion of the BDC linkers. However, it is impossible to distinguish between missing linker and missing cluster defects using TGA alone [78]. Chiranjib Gogoi et al [79] reported $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF could withstand $440~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The first stage is due to the weightlessness of water molecules. The second stage of weightlessness is due to the removal of guest molecules. The weightlessness of the third stage is due to the decomposition of ligands. When being excited, the catalytic stability of MIL-101 $(\mathsf{C r})$ was improved by phenylphosphonic acid (PPOA). Considering that PPOA-O possessed slightly higher porosity than PPOA-0.25, the enhanced catalytic activity of PPOA-0.25 contributed to its hierarchically porous structure which eventually accelerated the diffusion rate and increasingly exposed the coordination metal sites [80]. Moreover, the catalytic stability of MIL-101(Cr) with a hierarchically porous structure and the conversion of indene were significantly improved. In addition, the influence of coordination modulation on crystal stability is also worth studying. The investigation of surface charge is a means to study the stability of colloid. The surface measurement of the samples modulated by acetic acid spanned the broadest range from $-5{-}41~\mathrm{mV}.$ The acetic acid modulated the self-assembly of the crystal so that its surface is positively charged. As the concentration of acetic acid modulator is increased, more surface defects are incorporated to increase the surface charge and then the repulsion between neighboring nanoparticles.
## 4. Factors affecting the modulator
## 4.1. The length of the carboxylic acid chain
Most reported MOFs are confined to micropore range, thus, adsorption and catalytic capacity of MOFs related with molecular diffusion are limited by microporous structure. The coordination modulation self-assembled hierarchically porous MOFs (HPMOFs) is regarded as an effective way to solve the molecular diffusion [81,82]. Existing studies have shown that the pKa and coordination mode of the modulator are important factors which affect the self-assembly of MOFs. In the study of Zahn, G., et al. [83], they have employed water as solvent and discussed the effects of formic acid, acetic acid and propionic acid with different alkyl chain lengths, among which the formic acid modulator is the most obvious. Unlike 5 equiv of formic acid in DMF solvent, 30 equiv of formic acid is required in aqueous solution, indicating that more modulators are required in the aqueous solution. A modulation-based strategy with the acetic acid, octanoic acid, dodecanoic acid and palmitic acid as modulator, was applied to hierarchically porous MOFs [84]. The modulator plays a dual role: the carboxylic acid coordinates with the metal ion for the formation of metal-oxo clusters, and the alkyl chain creates the additional pore space (Fig. 11a-c). Specially, the length of carboxylic acid chain determined the additional pore structure in UiO-66 (Fig. 11d-f). Short-chain carboxylic acids represented by acetic acid were difficult to produce mesoporous structures. When the chain in carboxylic acid reaches 12 carbon atoms, the mesoporous and macroporous structure were identified from the enlarged $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ hysteresis loops.
Zhou et al [85] reported the coordination modulation of modulators with different carbon chain length, including propanoic acid (C3), hexanoic acid (C6), nonanoic acid (C9), and myristic acid (C14). The hierarchically porous PCN-250 can be obtained and the mesopore formation mechanism (modulating or templating) can be demonstrated. For the coordination modulation mechanism, mesoporous structure is generated by the spatial effect of monocarboxylic acid. In the templating based mechanism, the hydrophoric alkyl chains of the monocarboxylic acid molecules self-assemble into micellar structure as template, and then the vacancy of template is removed to obtain the mesoporous structure of MOFs, as shown in Fig. 12a. In the first step of the synthesis, mono-carboxylic acids were added to the mixture of MOF precursor. Next, acetic acid is used as an etchant to further regulate the pore size. The acetic acid served three purposes: 1) It acted as a replacement ligand for any monocoordinated long chain molecules; 2) It helped to get rid of the unreacted material; 3) It was served as an etchant to remove substances with low crystallinity [86]. Moreover, the concentration of modulator is also an influential factor that cannot be ignored. When the acid of choice is used in 0.7 M [PCN-250(C6-0.7 M) and PCN-250(C9-0.7 M)] or $1.4~\mathrm{M}$ [PCN-250(C6-1.4 M) and PCN-250(C9-1.4 M)] concentrations, the HP-MOF structure was confirmed from the ‘‘S-Shaped” isotherm in all generated samples (Fig. 12b and c). However, the microporous structure was identified from Type I isotherm in both PCN$250(\mathsf{C6}-2.1\ \mathrm{M})$ and PCN-250(C9-2.1 M) (Fig. 12d). The results show that different concentrations of preparation have an obvious effect on the pore structure.
Elham Bagherzadeh et al [87] studied the effect of formic acid and acetic acid on the Fe-MIL-88A. SEM images were used to characterize the effect of the modulator on the crystal size and morphology, showing that the increased amount of acetic acid improved the uniformity of the particles. The low modulator concentration induced the nonuniformity in particle size, which was attributed to the new nucleation. Because of its similar functionality with the linker, acetic acid as a capping agent to control the whole crystal growth. The inter-facial effect of acetic acid on crystal growth is derived from terminating the assembly of new SBUs to the previous formed particles. Contrarily, in the case of formic acid, dendritic aggregated particles are formed, which was attributed to the DMF/water reaction medium [88].
## 4.2. Effect of acidity of modulator
Since Behren’s pioneering study of the coordination modulation effects of acetic acid and benzoic acid on the self-assembly of UiO66 and UiO-67 was reported [89], a variety of modulators such as trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) $\mathrm{(pK_{a}}=0.3\$ ), hydrochloride acid (HCl), formic acid (FA) $(\mathrm{pK}_{\mathrm{a}}=3.74^{\cdot}$ ), or even water have been investigated. In the study of Vakili, R. et al.[90], $40\mathrm{mol}$ equivalent BenAc and $185\mathrm{mol}$ equivalent HCl were considered to be the optimal dosage of the modulator, indicating that the dosage of modulators with varied acidity was different. Hu et al [91] conducted an extensive study on the effects of different acidity modulator including acetic acid, formic acid and trifluoroacetic acid on the water-based synthesis of UiO-66-type MOFs. The acidity of the modulators was in the order of TFA $(0.3)>\mathrm{FA}(3.74)>\mathrm{AA}(4$ .76). It was reported that the acidity and type of the modulator affect the morphology, BET, pore size and thermodynamic stability of the UiO-66, as shown in Fig. 13 a-d.
Stable colloidal nanoparticles matter has a wide range of applications in the field of material self-assembly, adsorption and catalysis. Specially, the influence of the acidity and concentration of the modulator on the surface charge of UiO-66 nanoparticles was studied, which is closely related to the stability of colloids. The colloidal stability of each MOF was evaluated by virtue of dynamic light scattering, which was found to be dependent on the lower pKa and high concentration of the modulator, corresponding to the improved polydispersity [92]. The modulator including acetic acid (AA), formic acid (FA), dichloroacetic acid (DCA), and trifluoroacetic acid (TFA) could effectively modulate the size of nanoparticles ranging from 17(2)-721(56) nm, 17(4)-1174 (258) nm, 19(5)-550 (51), to 38(7)-227 (46) nm respectively. Both metal coordination, the pKa of the ligand and the modulator and the pH of solution affected the self-assembly of the MOFs. In particular, the selection of an appropriate modulator should be carefully considered [93].
## 4.3. The concentration of the modulator
Both the acidity of the modulator and the concentration of the modulator affect the crystal structure of the MOFs. Different amounts of citric acid (CA) were introduced into the synthetic of HKUST-1 in the ethanol solvent. When citric acid was raised to $0.24{\mathrm{~mL}}$ , the sub-micrometer HKUST-1 octahedral particles with concave facets were obtained. Based on the integrity of the crystal, the critic acid competes with the BTC ligand (BTC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5- tricarboxylic acid), which was conducive to the formation of HKUST-1 defect and macroporous and mesoporous structure [94].
The effect of benzoic acid on the crystal morphology and size of UiO-66 was clearly visible [95]. After adding more benzoic acid, the diffraction peak width of the crystal was narrowed, indicating the reduced agglomeration of the crystal. Benzoic acid modulator also had the same effect on UiO-66-NDC (1,4-naphthalenedicarboxilic acid) and UiO-66 isomer as reported by Butova, V. V., et al [96]. It emerged that the high content of modulator was beneficial to crystal formation with definite octahedral morphology. When the dosage of the modulator was 60 benzoic acid (BC), the octahedral shape particles have a uniform size. The shifting degree of pcutype interpenetrated framework from NJU-Bai1 to NJU-Bai2 was well controlled by L-tyrosine modulator concentration. This significantly contributed to the improved hydrogen adsorption properties [97]. The effects of modulator on crystal structure and properties in Zr-based, Fe-based, Cu-based MOFs was shown in Table 1.
## 4.4. Solvent effect
The effect of solvent and dodecanoic acid on the morphology and crystallinity of HKUST-1 was investigated. When the dodecanoic acid was applied to the synthesis of HKUST-1, the solvent effect was also taken into account [98]. It was found that with an increasing dielectric constant (Ɛ’) and increasing loss factor (Ɛ’’), the obviously enhanced diffraction peak intensity of the crystalline cubic {111} plane in Cu-BTC was identified, suggesting that the important effects of the dielectric constant and dielectric loss constant of the solvent. This behavior could be deemed as a result of dielectric dissipation of microwave-induced energy because of dipole rotation and ionic conduction of molecules in the reaction medium. The addition of n-dodecanoic acid is necessary for emergence of Cu-BTC nanocrystals, and its morphology changed from octahedron to hexahedron, which is due to n-dodecanoic acid acting as a capping agent during crystal growth.
High-resolution neutron diffraction and single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) are means to identify missing-linker defects by the modulator. The disordered arrangement of crystal defects and the low electron density limit the accessibility of ordinary diffraction techniques. In the work of Fu et al.[99], by combining experimental solid state nuclear magnetic resonance (SSNMR) techniques with computational methods, they successfully revealed the complex behavior of residual acetate modulators in MOF defects.
It is reasonable to resort to the isotopic enrichment of $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ spins of carboxyl functional group in an acetic acid molecule. It is confirmed by $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ SSNMR spectrum, the acetate molecules undergo a fast but non-isotropic motion, which gave rise to the average chemical shift anisotropy (CSA) patterns. The fact that more residual water can stabilize the defect-associated acetate was confirmed by the experiment of kinetics of acetate-water exchange. A temperature of $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ facilitated the removal of acetate from the defect in the form of acetic acid. The study provides a comprehensive understanding of the acetate, water, and the dynamic equilibrium between the two associated with defect formation. It is found that acetate molecules coordinate to a single metal site monodentately and pair with water at the neighboring position. The highly flexibility of acetate molecules undergoes slow kinetic exchange with water through dynamic hydrogen bonds, which provides important information for accurate detection of defective MOF structures with intrinsic dynamic and disorder flexibility. In addition to $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ SSNMR, the dissolution $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR analysis is credible to identify the ratio of the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -bdc linker and the acetic acid modulator coordinated to the Zr-oxo clusters in defective UiO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Another representative work is that the interdependence of modulator, linker, and solvent amounts in the synthesis of the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF [100]. $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}\mathrm{:}H_{2}b p d c\mathrm{:}H_{2}O\mathrm{:}D M F}$ ratios of 1:1:3:50 (concentrated synthesis) or 1:1:3:300 (dilute synthesis) as starting conditions was compared. Due to the effect of the coexisting hydrogen ions, the preparation further reduces the solubility of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b p d c}$ , and the hydrolysis of the solvent introduced $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ into the solution, causing the deprotonation of formic acid competing with ligands for Zr sites. Deprotonated ligands, modulation agents and hydrolyzed solvents, and metal clusters co-exist in the self-assembly of the UiO-67 (Fig. 14a). Importantly, as shown in Fig. 14b, the number of linker defects can be determined by the amount of solvent to maintain the saturation concentration of linker during the synthesis.
## 5. The effect of modulator on crystal properties
## 5.1. The effect of modulator on adsorbability
The reasons of the effect of the modulator on the adsorption capacity of the MOFs can be summarized as follows: firstly, it affects the crystal growth kinetics; secondly, it affects the pore structure; thirdly, it affects the surface charge distribution of the crystal. In 2011, Behrens and co-workers reported the selfassembled coordination modulation of UiO-66 by monocarboxylic acid [5]. Commonly used monocarboxylic acids involved formic (FA), trifluroacetic and benzoic acids (TFA) as well as hydrochloric acid (HCl). Besides inherent defects, missing linker defects and missing cluster defects coexist in $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ MOFs. The concentration, porosity, and location of defects are affected by the concentration and type of the modulator. Either the defect increased the hydrophobicity of MOFs, which is reflected in the increased adsorption capacity of organic pollutants [101]. The defects makes hydroxy, water and carboxylic acid to act as capping agent. It is reasonable to infer that the adsorption capacity of MOFs to anions can be improved by adjusting the defect concentration. In the study of Li et al. [102], experimental findings as well as theoretical density functional theory (DFT) calculations disclosed that small compensation ligands (Cl-, $\mathsf{H O}^{-}$ , or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ) are more likely to undergo ligand exchange reactions than large compensation ligands $(0_{2}C-$ $\mathrm{CH}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{O}_{2}\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-CF}_{3}^{-}$ or ${\sf O}_{2}{\sf C}{\sf-C}{\sf H}{\sf F}_{2}^{-}$ ). Meanwhile, the influence of defect structure on anion adsorption can be explained by the crystal asymmetry obtained from computational chemistry. The uptake of Se (VI) oxyanions toward defective UiO-66 was based on the substitution of capping ligands by Se (VI) oxyanions. The Zr-O or $Z\mathrm{r-Cl}$ distance increases gradually in the order of UiO-66-Ac $\prec$ UiO66-Dif $\angle\cdot\angle$ UiO-66-Trif $\angle{\mathbf{\Omega}}$ UiO-66-HCl. As shown in Fig. 15a, the adsorption of Se (VI) oxyanions makes the structure more stable due to the reduced $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ bond length. As shown in Fig. 15b, the existence of capping agent and adsorption of selenium and oxygen anion caused the change of electrostatic potential. Liu et al [93] found that the acetic acid modulated UiO-66 has good a desulfurization performance. Consistent with the study of Liu et al.[103], compared with benzoic acid, hydrochloric acid has a stronger modulated effect on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -UiO-67. Similarly, 3 eq. Benzoic acid (benzoic acid $/Z\mathrm{r}C\vert_{4}=3\$ ) played an important role in the improved adsorption capacity of Zr-fum for arsenite and arsenate from aqueous solution [104].
The roles of the modulator (benzoic acid, BenAc and hydrochloric acid) in the increased adsorption capacity are also prominent [95].
This may be due to two main reasons: (i) The modulator increased the specific surface area and the pore volume of UiO-67, which resulted in more adsorption sites due to linker deficiency; and (ii) The modulator affected the nucleation quantity and nucleation time [105]. Hu et al. [106] examined the adsorptive performance of $Z\mathrm{r/Hf-}$ fum was affected by acetic acid, formic acid, trifluoroacetic acid. In particular, the attention was paid to the correlation between the dosage of the modulator and the adsorption performance [106]. The sulfone-functionalized Zr UiO-67 and Hf UiO-67 MOFs with hierarchical mesopores were successfully synthesized using the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dibenzoic acid- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -sulfone ligand, with acetic acid (AcOH) or HCl as the modulator [107]. The significant structural features of AcO- ion and $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ coordination can be confirmed in $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectroscopy. The large amount of stochiometric defect in the presence of AcOH, is expected to create hierarchical mesoporosity. Control over missing linker defects using AcOH and HCl makes the adjustable hierarchical mesoporosity possible. In addition, the available organic linkers functionalized with sulfone groups, is essential to increase the adsorption capacity of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ .
## 5.2. The effect of modulator on catalytic activity
MOFs have great applications in catalysis for their special active sites, high porosity, large specific surface area, and adjustable pore size. However, the preparation of MOFs catalytic materials with high catalytic activity and the expansion of its catalytic application system are the difficulties and hotspots in the catalytic field at present. The key to the improved catalytic activity of the modulator lies in the production of additional Lewis acid sites and more open structures. In 2013, Vermoortele, F., et al. [108] demonstrated that the unexpected catalytic activity of the seemingly coordinatively saturated UiO-66 is due to missing linkers in the structure. Trifluroacetic acid replaces some of the linker of terephthalic acid in the material, creates additional Lewis acid sites and a more open skeleton structure, and improves the catalytic activity of zirconium terephthalate for multiple reactions. The thermal activation process of materials is indispensable. TFA affects the dehydroxylation of UiO-66 through dehydration, which not only leads to the dehydroxylation of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}_{6}}$ cluster, but also leads to the removal of the trifluoroacetic acid group after synthesis, thus forming a more open framework with a large number of open sites. As the amount of TFA increased, the intensity of $Z\Gamma{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}_{2}$ and $Z\mathrm{r-OH}$ gradually increased, while the intensity of $\upmu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ decreased, the results show that TFA can effectively adjust the amount of hydroxyl as the catalytic active sites. In addition, the direct catalytic synthesis of DMC (dimethyl carbonate) by UiO-66 modulated by TFA is a direct verification of the increased concentration of missing-linker defect. As the terminal hydroxide/water $\left(\mathbf{M}{-}0\mathbf{H}/\mathbf{M}{-}0\mathbf{H}_{2}\right)^{.}$ of defective $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}/$ Hf6 nodes in $Z\mathrm{r}$ -UiO-67 (BA) and Zr-UiO-67 (HCl) can act as Brønsted acid catalysts, such as the catalytic oxidation of styrene. The Lewis acid and base site was successfully introduced into JLU-MOF 58 by acetic acid modulator. In JLU-MOF58, carboxylic acid groups occupy eight edges of the $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ octahedral cluster, and the remaining active sites are terminated by hydroxyl and water molecules. The catalytic performance of JLU-MOF 58 was excellent for cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with co-catalyst oftetra-n-tertbutylammonium bromide (TBAB) [109]. The reduction in connectivity leads to more Lewis and Brønsted acid sites exposed and more exposure to the reactants.
Moreover, the modulator affects the hydrophilicity of the MOFs, which is closely related to the defect sites, thus affecting the catalytic activity of MOFs. It was found that the defect induced hydrophilicity is quantified in terms of Henry’s constant and saturated water absorption [110]. Instead of attacking smaller pores, TFA enlarges double cavities simultaneously. Compared with AA modulated sample, the TFA modulated sample has a higher Henry constant. This might be attributed to the addition of AA which not only replaces the BDC ligand, but also acts as a terminating group for the missing linkers. The bigger cavity in TFA modulated UiO-66 makes zirconium metal sites exposed to water and structural rearrangement, leading to water-induced missing linker.
So far, the self-assembly of functionalized UiO-66 influenced by modulators has been rarely reported. In 2012, the Vermoortele, F., et al [111] reported that the nitro-substituted UiO-66 has stronger characteristics of Lewis acid. In addition, the electron-withdrawing nitro group has a strong interaction with the hydrogen atoms in the transition state and the adsorbed product state. Thus, the effect of modulators on the functional groups, including Gibbs energyfree and transition state, is a fascinating subject. Notably, the missing linker termination was also affected by the synthesis condition, and the one in functional Zr-based MOFs is more abundant, which needs to be identified by a combination of multiple characterization methods. Recently, Jrad, A., et al.[112] investigated the effects of acidity and concentration of formic acid and acetic acid modulators on catalytic activity, in the final analysis, which is related with the number of missing ligand, specific surface area and particle size in UiO-66, UiO-66 $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{\mathsf{C O O H}})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ (Fig. 16a-d). The growth of the crystal was due to the slowing down of the crystallization process deriving from the ’’competition’’ between the modulator and the organic ligand to bind with the SBUs. The particles size of nanoparticles followed the order of UiO-66 $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}<\mathrm{UiO}{-}66<\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2}),$ which depends on the highly acidic ligand with a decisive competitive advantage over the modulator.
The acid modulation strategy is effective to introduce defect sites as active catalytic sites. Lauric acid has obvious influence on the catalytic properties of thermal plasma [113]. The lauric acid could not completely replace the low acidity carboxylate ligand $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ , while the remaining alkyl chain creates structural defects, which facilitates the formation of additional pore spaces. It is the formation of this special structure and the nanosize of particle that are conducive to the improvement of catalytic activity. In 2021, the regulation of MIL-53(Fe) by acetic acid as a catalyst for the selective oxidation of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}$ was reported [114]. The structural defects are induced by the competitive exchange between different acidity $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ linker $\left(\mathrm{pK}_{\mathrm{a}}=3.51\right.$ ) and HAC modulator $(\mathrm{pK_{a}}=4.76),$ . Derived from the instability and volatility of acetic acid, vacuum thermal activation could make more $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ sites active. The $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ Lewis acid sites was reduced to $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ by the $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ molecules binding on the surface of the MIL-53(Fe). Then, $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ is oxidized to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ by oxidation molecules, participating in the reaction through recycling.
## 5.3. Effects of modulators on photoelectric properties of MOFs
The influence of modulators on the photoelectric properties of MOFs was in its infancy. When HCl was used as modulator, the electrochemical properties of a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -doped Ni-based MOF with honeycomblike hierarchical spherical structure was excellent and could be used as a candidate for electrode materials in supercapacitors [115]. The study focuses on the effect of both the modulator and the doped zinc ions on chemical properties. The important roles of the modulator were revealed by the following aspects: (1) Morphology control is important. Porous honeycomblike hierarchical structure is conducive to the improvement of the photoelectric properties. (2) It is important to form crystal structures that facilitate electron transfer. The chains of nickel octahedra connected to each other offer a conductive channel for electrons, and it is beneficial to the diffusion of electrolyte solution by the parallel layers with interspaces. (3) The influence of synthetic environment cannot be ignored. The acidic environment is conducive to the formation of cluster missing defects and creates additional pore space for charge transfer and enhancement of photoelectric properties. In the work of Zhou et al. [116], acetic acid (AA) and HCl were selected as the modulators. The Ni-MOF-AA-180 $(180^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ showed opened hollow urchin-like microspheres with a large crystalline size, and the Ni-MOF- ${\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{-}180$ $(180^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ appeared as a microsphere with smooth surface. Compared with the HCl modulated Ni-MOFs, acetic acid modulated Ni-MOFs have better crystallinity and specific surface area, which are conducive to electron transfer and the intercalation/deintercalation of electrolyte ions. The modulator strategy is also effective for improving the electronic properties of thin film. The basic modulator pyridine and the acid modulator acetic acid work together to control the layer-by-layer (LbL) fabrication of $\mathsf{\{C u_{2}(b d c)_{2}(b p y)\}_{n}}$ thin films from different directions [117]. The essence of photoelectric property is the proton conductivity. Jared M. Taylor et al [118] demonstrated that through the introduction of long-chain fatty acid, the proton conductivity of UiO-66 was increased 3 orders of magnitude to $6.79\times10^{-3}\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ On the one hand, the Lewis acid sites generated by ligand defects provided flowing protons which coordinated with water molecules, while the increased pore volume provided channels for proton migration. On the other hand, the proton mobility is a key factor in improving conductivity. In addition, ammonia acts as a modulator can effectively increase the conductivity of a copperbased graphene-like framework by fewer stacked layers [119]. The measured high conductivity value of $0.045\ \mathrm{S\cm}^{-1}$ makes thin film possible to act as a core component of photoelectric devices.
The effect of the modulator on the photocatalytic activity of MOFs is mainly related to the transformed crystal structure and the photoelectron transition energy level. The work of Jiang et al [120] reported hydrochloric acid modulator was used to modulate the self-assembly process and photocatalytic activity of MIL-53 (Fe). The HCl modulator as deprotonizing agent effectively controlled the morphology and size of the crystal, and the mesoporous structure generated by the modulation was conducive to the enhancement of visible light absorption capacity, photoelectron excitation and transition. Compared with the MIL-53(Fe), the photocatalytic activity of acid-regulated MIL-53 was increased by 1.5 times. In the study of M. Campanelli et al.[121], 8.75 equiv acetic acid was required for solvent-free self-assembly synthesis and energy level modulation for Ce-UiO-66 MOFs, which has strong homologies with Zr-based UiO-66 MOFs. Acetic acid modulation affects the visible light absorption capacity and the band gap of Ce-UiO-66, which was attributed to the modulator competing with the linker that induced a different LMCT. Due to the electronwithdrawing effect of the aromatic substituent, the MOFs displayed good photocatalytic properties toward the photooxidation of substituted benzylic alcohols to aldehydes.
More and more attention has been paid to the effect of modulators on the functional MOFs. In 2017, Hu et al [122] reported the crystal size was affected by pseudolinker acid (p-toluylic acid, benzoic acid), and the crystal morphology of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 was mainly controlled by the small molecular carboxylic acid represented by acetic acid and thioglycolic acid. PTA (p-toluylic acid) acts like a pseudolinker, which differed from the original linker $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC in having a carboxyl group. Due to the similarity of structure, organic ligands can be partially replaced by PTA, resulting in a nucleation rate and more crystal seeds. Meanwhile, PTA modulated the rich hierarchical structure, meaning the coexistence of a large scale mesopore and macropore. The regulation principle of acetic acid is different, in the first stage, acetic acid acts as a ligand competitor and inhibits the growth of crystals to a large scale. Moreover, excessive acetic acid gets stuck to the {110} facet of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL125 crystals, limiting the crystal formation of octahedral morphology. Interestingly, compared with small molecular acid samples (HAc-8 and HS-8) (acetic acid and thioglycolic acid $=8$ equiv), the photocatalytic degradation capacity of the pseudolinker acid samples (PTA-8 and BA-8) (p-toluylic acid, benzoic acid $=8$ equiv) has been greatly improved, showing that the pore structure plays a greater role in promoting photocatalytic activity than the morphology. Compared with the material without HCl, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ materials modulated by HCl with defects exhibit an excellent ability of photocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The defect engineering makes the active sites more readily available and more open frameworks, and slows down the effective photogentic charge recombination, and improves the photocatalytic activity [123]. In 2019, Jiang et al [124] paid special attention to UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ modulated by acetic acid modulator. It is confirmed that the removal of linkers in UiO-66 lowers energy levels of the unoccupied dorbitals of Zr atoms, which may increase the likelihood of charge transfer in the photocatalytic process. Ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy can be used to characterize relaxation dynamics and charge separation efficiency, and the $\mathrm{JiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}1$ 00 (acetic acid $=100$ equiv) with moderate structural defects modulated by modulator possesses the highest charge separation efficiency and the most outstanding relaxation kinetics.
In summary, the influence of the modulator on the photoelectric properties of the MOFs is concentrated on the following aspects: firstly, increased specific surface area and layered structure are conducive to the contact between the material and the reaction site; secondly, defect sites can accelerate transmission of proton; thirdly, modulators are conducive to the prolonging of the photogenic carrier life.
## 5.4. The effect of modulator on fluorescence properties
The synthesis time and fluorescence detection efficiency of JLUMOF50 $\mathrm{'L}=\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{MDCPB}=5^{\prime}$ -methyl- $[1,1^{\prime},3^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $\cdot4,4^{\prime\prime}$ -dicar boxylic acid) were shortened by formic acid modulator [125]. The presence of modulator significantly increased the tolerance and stability of JLU-MOF to acid and base, unfortunately, the qualitative relationship between the modulator dosage and fluorescence detection characteristics was unrevealed. The addition of benzoic acid modulator to the $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -based MOF foundation has obvious fluorescence detection characteristics. Hydrofluoric acid (HF) as a modulator is selective for the nanoparticle morphology and nanorod morphology of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al). The double role of HF was revealed. On the one hand, hydrofluoric acid, as a weak acid, affects the deprotonation of the ligand due to its relatively strong binding ability between hydrogen and fluorine atoms. On the other hand, the growth of the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was inhibited because the $\mathsf{F}^{-}$ ion by virtue of the high electronegativity competed with $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ for coordination with $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ . $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) nanorods can be used to build photoluminescence humidity sensors. On the other hand, due to its strong electronegativity, fluoride ion competes with $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC ligand for the $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ coordination site, impacting the construction of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) topological structure. The shrinkage of the framework occurred because the abundant water molecules gather in the micropores of the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) nanorods and bridge H–O H–N hydrogen bonding with ligands. The reduction of ligand spacing increases the accumulation and intensifies of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking and finally induces the enhanced fluorescence effect. The symmetry of Mg-based MOFs was also affected by acetic acid modulator. SNNU-35 $(\mathrm{L}=\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB},4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime})$ -benzene-1,3,5-triben zoic acid) and SNNU-36 $\mathrm{'}\mathrm{L}=\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{FD}\mathsf{A},2,5$ -furandicarboxylic acid), as the representative of Mg-based MOFs, were prepared by acetic acid modulator. By virtue of the electron-rich effect and the porous proton channel, two Mg-based MOFs can be selected as effective quantitative fluorescent probes for the detection of nitroaromatic compounds [126]. The effect of the modulation on the fluorescence properties may be related to the size and morphology of crystal. The $\mathtt{E u}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Tb}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{-MOF}$ thin film constructed from nanocrystals by sodium acetate exhibited excellent fluorescence detection ability.
As a deprotonation agent and size modulator, the base modulated synthesis of NMOFs with uniform size is very important to improve the fluorescence detection performance. The uniform microplates of Zn-CP (ligand: 5-(3-pyridyl)-1,3,4-oxadiazole-2-thi ol (Hpzt) were obtained with the addition of pyridine, an organic weak base, acting as a size modulator and a deprotonating reagent. The fluorescence sensitivity of Zn-CP microplate to TNP (Trinitrophenols) was improved by pyridine because pyridine created uncoordinated Lewis basic sites in the layers [127]. Urea modulated $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ - MIL-53(Al) by virtue of the plate shape morphology and nanometer size has a good fluorescence detection characteristic of free chlorine. A possible mechanism of energy transfer through N-H O-Cl hydrogen bonding constructed between free chlorine and the amine groups attached to $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) nanoplates was proposed.
However, the studies on the effect of modulator on the fluorescence properties are at an initial stage. The modulation mechanism is largely unknown. Meanwhile, it is not certain whether molecules are influenced by excited electron energy level transitions or vibrational and rotational energy level transitions by modulators. Also, little is known about how ligand to metal charge transfer (LMCT) process is influenced by the modulator. Furthermore, whether or not the modulators affect the growth of some crystal faces, which is related to the fluorescence properties is still a mystery. Clearly, all the above merit further investigation in the near future.
## 6. Conclusions and future challenges
(1) The modulator plays an important role of capping agent in the assembly of MOFs, and it is generally believed that the missing carboxylic acid group $(\mathsf{R C O}^{2-})$ will be replaced by a pair of hydroxide and water molecules. The determination of the compensation site of ligand defect requires more advanced characterization techniques. Defect sites have been widely used in catalyst and Lewis acid catalysis, and the characterization of acidity strength of defect sites lacks accurate technology.
(2) The action of coordination modulator is not only affected by acidity, concentration and solvent ratio, but also by pH of solution, synthesis temperature and synthesis time, etc. Therefore, comprehensive analysis of influencing factors of crystal nucleation growth is very important.
(3) The influence of existing modulators on the self-assembly of crystals is mainly concentrated on the stable UiO-66. However, the types of MOFs vary greatly. The influence of modulators on crystal engineering and defect engineering should involve more types of MOFs.
(4) The effect of the addition order of modulators on the crystal structure and properties is also an interesting research topic. Modulators either were added in the precursor mixture solution, or added in the post-synthesis process. Very likely, the addition order influenced the adsorption and the catalytic sites.
(5) The combination of experiment and computational means provides more options for revealing the intermediate states of the crystal, the free energy of the possible crystal structure, the adsorptive binding energy of the crystal surface, and the construction of the connectivity model.
(6) COFs (Covalent Organic Frameworks) are important part of porous crystal materials. Coordination modulators have been reported to participate the self-assembly of COFs to regulate the crystallinity and porosity. A mono-functional boronic acid modulator was selected to adjust the crystallinity and porosity of COFs [128]. The thiol and carboxylic acid functional groups were integrated into the COF structure and provided options for further synthetic modifications. Castano et al [129] studied the nucleation kinetics in the supplement of 4-tert-butylcatechol as a competitor in boronate ester-linked COFs and discovered that the competitor could effectively suppress colloid nucleation, enabling anisotropic growth of COFs at high monomer concentrations. Yaghi et al. [119] demonstrated the imine functionalization of COFs can be realized by adding aniline in the condensation process of aromatic amine and aldehydes. Verduzco et al. [131] reported that benzaldehyde is an effective modulator for enhancing the crystallinity and porosity of imine-linked covalent organic frameworks (COFs). The influence of coordination preparation on the self-assembly process of COFs is just at the initial stage [130], and the influence of coordination preparation on crystal properties has not been investigated, which could be the focus of the research in the next stage.
(7) The influence of the modulator on the photoelectric properties of the MOFs is still at the initial stage. The influence of the modulator on the visible light absorption capacity, the excitation and migration of photogenerated electrons, and the bandgap width in MOFs are all confused, and the influence of the modulator on proton and electronic transition is not clear. More research is needed to understand the effects of modulators on both charge transfer and energy transfer from ligand to metal. More advanced technology is needed to solve the mystery.
Although the coordination modulation strategy is widely applicable to most MOFs there are still many opportunities to develop coordination modulation in the future, especially, for highvalence state metals and some MOFs with photoelectric properties. A better understanding of various driving forces in the process of MOFs autonomous assembly, though still random in nature, is of great significance for the design optimization and application of MOFs.
